ilience the grittiness to go  forward and lead but does it tell you  more than success does I do you think  both of them are really important and  one of the things I talk about in the  book is like you have to understand what  blows your hair back like where are you  super in your element being your best  and that's when you've been successful  but also the failures help you refine to  figure out where that place is  and don't make yourself obsolete yeah  yeah about that regardless of your age  you said yeah everybody has something to  offer absolutely absolutely and I I talk  at the end about like break yourself to  make yourself like  as soon as you get to the point where  you feel like you're at the top of your  game that's a good time to explore do  something new that's one of the things I  learned from definitely all the people  what are you can do with blind will why  will is the most awesome like indoor  cycling company I call that pitch  fitness for extremists because it's all  about what we have is this technology  that enables you as you're riding to see  your score on a board and like to  compare your results to yourself and so  where this is the beginning of how we  can take back technology to all sorts of  other places to allow lots of other  people to participate in flywheel thank  you for coming thank you yeah Rob  O'Hagan remember that name the name of  the book by the way it's called extreme  you she said we all have some extremity  in us somewhere sometimes you gotta dig  deep it's on sale right now wherever you  like to buy your books
 

